{"cuisine/hlai-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/hlai-cuisine","n":"Hlai cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in China","intro":[],"src":null,"wiki":"Hlai cuisine","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"hlai-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hlai_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/indo-fijian-food":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/indo-fijian-food","n":"Indo-Fijian food","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Diaspora cuisine in Fiji","intro":[],"src":null,"wiki":"Indo-Fijian food","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"indo-fijian-food"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indo-Fijian_food"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Indo Fijian Recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"indofijianrecipes.blogspot.com","note":"Blog of home-style Indo-Fijian curries, chutneys and sweets.","url":"https://indofijianrecipes.blogspot.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Annapurna: Gastronomic Delights from My Fiji Indian Childhood","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Nalini Naidu","note":"Fiji Indian family recipes and childhood memories.","url":"","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/dine-foodways":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/dine-foodways","n":"Diné foodways","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]],"sub":"Indigenous foodways, Navajo Nation, Arizona, New Mexico and Utah","names":"Diné","intro":[["Diné people live across the Navajo Nation in the Four Corners, and corn is at the heart of the table. ",["Blue corn","ingredient/blue-corn"]," is cooked into mush, dumplings and bread, often with juniper ash stirred in, and fresh corn is ground, wrapped in husks and baked in a pit as ",["Kneel down bread","dish/kneel-down-bread"],". Gardens grow corn, beans, squash and melons, and families travel to gather wild sumac berries, chokecherries and banana yucca."],["Sheep have been part of Diné life since the 1600s, so much that a well known Diné saying calls sheep life itself. ",["Mutton","ingredient/lamb"]," stew with potatoes, carrots and sometimes ",["Hominy","ingredient/hominy"]," is a classic, served with ",["Frybread","dish/frybread"]," or tortillas. Flour, sugar and coffee came after 1868 and are still in Diné kitchens, while summits and cooking classes pass the older recipes to younger cooks."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"Inside Climate News, Navajo food gathering summit","url":"https://insideclimatenews.org/news/07122024/navajo-food-gathering-summit/"},{"name":"NPR, To get calcium, Navajos burn juniper branches to eat the ash","url":"https://www.npr.org/sections/thesalt/2017/08/21/544191316/to-get-calcium-navajos-burn-juniper-branches-to-eat-the-ash"},{"name":"Bureau of Indian Education, Indigenous Food Hubs Harvest of the Month, mutton","url":"https://www.bie.edu/sites/default/files/documents/harvest_of_the_month_dec_2025_mutton.pdf"},{"name":"Santa Fe New Mexican, kneel down bread","url":"https://www.santafenewmexican.com/pasatiempo/comidas-y-mas-kneel-down-bread-recipe/article_32562c74-385e-11ee-a720-dbdff5e25ab9.html"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dine-foodways"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":3,"links":[["Blue corn mush","dish/blue-corn-mush"],["Frybread","dish/frybread"],["Kneel down bread","dish/kneel-down-bread"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"New Native Kitchen: Celebrating Modern Recipes of the American Indian","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Freddie Bitsoie and James O. Fraioli, 2021","note":"Modern Native recipes from chef Freddie Bitsoie, who draws on his Navajo background.","url":"https://www.publishersweekly.com/9781419753558","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781419753558"}]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Blue corn","ingredient/blue-corn"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Lard","ingredient/lard"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Maize","ingredient/maize"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/8/8c/Frybread.jpg/500px-Frybread.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Unknown author","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frybread.jpg","of":"Frybread"}},"ingredient/amala":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/amala","n":"Amala","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Àmàlà is a staple okele or swallow food originating from and popularized by the Yoruba ethnic group of southwestern Nigeria and other parts of Yorubaland. It is made of yam, cassava flour, or unripe plantain flour. Tubers of yams are peeled, sliced, cleaned, dried and then ground into flour. It is also called èlùbọ́. Yams are white in colour but turn brown when dried which gives àmàlà its colour. It is a popular side dish served with ewédú and gbẹ̀gìrì (black-eyed beans soup), but it is also served with a variety of other ọbè (soups), such as ẹ̀fọ́, ilá, and ogbono."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-amala"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amala_(food)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Yoruba cuisine","cuisine/yoruba-cuisine",[["Amala (food)","dish/amala-food"]]]]},{