Onion","ingredient/onion"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/f/f7/01_Mmm..._Apple_Crisp_with_Whipped_Cream.jpg/500px-01_Mmm..._Apple_Crisp_with_Whipped_Cream.jpg","w":500,"h":345,"by":"jeffreyw","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:01_Mmm..._Apple_Crisp_with_Whipped_Cream.jpg"}},"dish/basler-lackerli":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/basler-lackerli","n":"Basler Läckerli","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Swiss cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["The Basler Läckerli (also Leckerli or Läggerli, lecker meaning \"delicious\" in German and -li being a diminutive suffix) is a traditional hard spice biscuit originating from Basel, Switzerland. It is made of honey, hazelnuts, almonds, candied peel, and Kirsch. After baking in a thin layer, the still hot dough is topped with a sugar glaze and cut into rectangular pieces."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-basler-lackerli"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basler_L%C3%A4ckerli"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Swiss cuisine","cuisine/swiss-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":7,"links":[["Almond","ingredient/almond"],["Candied fruit","ingredient/candied-fruit"],["Candied peel","ingredient/candied-peel"],["Hazelnut","ingredient/hazelnut"],["Honey","ingredient/honey"],["Kirsch","ingredient/kirsch"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Helvetic Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"helvetickitchen.com","note":"Andie Pilot's blog of Swiss home cooking and baking, with notes on Swiss cookbooks.","url":"https://www.helvetickitchen.com/recipes/retrospective","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Cuisine Helvetica","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"cuisinehelvetica.com","note":"Heddi Nieuwsma's Swiss recipes and food culture from her life in Switzerland.","url":"https://cuisinehelvetica.com/swiss-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Swiss cuisine","links":[["Älplermagronen","dish/alplermagronen"],["Birnbrot","dish/birnbrot"],["Bündnerfleisch","dish/bundnerfleisch"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Carac (pastry)","dish/carac-pastry"],["Cervelat","dish/cervelat"],["Cholera (food)","dish/cholera-food"],["Egg puffs and soup pearls","dish/egg-puffs-and-soup-pearls"],["Landjäger","dish/landjager"],["Leberkäse","dish/leberkase"],["Raclette","dish/raclette"],["Riz Casimir","dish/riz-casimir"],["Schenkele","dish/schenkele"],["Spätzle","dish/spatzle"],["Swiss sausages and cured meats","dish/swiss-sausages-and-cured-meats"],["Wähe","dish/wahe"],["Zopf","dish/zopf"],["Zuger Kirschtorte","dish/zuger-kirschtorte"],["Zürcher Geschnetzeltes","dish/zurcher-geschnetzeltes"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/5b/Basler_L%C3%A4ckerli_01.jpeg/500px-Basler_L%C3%A4ckerli_01.jpeg","w":500,"h":330,"by":"Manuel Schneider(bla) (+/-)","lic":"CC BY 2.5","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basler_L%C3%A4ckerli_01.jpeg"}},"ingredient/phaseolus-vulgaris":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/phaseolus-vulgaris","n":"Phaseolus vulgaris","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 5 dishes across 7 cuisines","intro":[["Phaseolus vulgaris, the common bean, is a herbaceous annual plant. Its botanical classification, along with other Phaseolus species, is as a member of the legume family, Fabaceae. It forms a green-leaved vine which produces beans inside of pods."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-phaseolus-vulgaris"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phaseolus_vulgaris"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":7,"groups":[["Portuguese cuisine","cuisine/portuguese-cuisine",[["Cocido","dish/cocido"],["Feijoada","dish/feijoada"]]],["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine",[["Caldo galego","dish/caldo-galego"],["Cocido","dish/cocido"]]],["Brazilian cuisine","cuisine/brazilian-cuisine",[["Feijoada","dish/feijoada"]]],["Cypriot cuisine","cuisine/cypriot-cuisine",[["Fasolada","dish/fasolada"]]],["Galician cuisine","cuisine/galician-cuisine",[["Caldo galego","dish/caldo-galego"]]],["Greek cuisine","cuisine/greek-cuisine",[["Fasolada","dish/fasolada"]]],["Tuscan cuisine","cuisine/tuscan-cuisine",[["Ribollita","dish/ribollita"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Bacon","ingredient/bacon"],["Cabbage","ingredient/cabbage"],["Chorizo","ingredient/chorizo"],["Lacón","ingredient/lacon"],["Pig fat","ingredient/pig-fat"],["Rapini","ingredient/rapini"],["White bean","ingredient/white-bean"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Lunch meat","ingredient/lunch-meat"],["Salo","ingredient/salo"],["Common bean","ingredient/common-bean"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Common beans","ingredient/common-beans"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/c/ca/Snijboon_peulen_Phaseolus_vulgaris.jpg/500px-Snijboon_peulen_Phaseolus_vulgaris.jpg","w":500,"h":667,"by":"Rasbak","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Snijboon_peulen_Phaseolus_vulgaris.jpg"}},"dish/calentao":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/calentao","n":"Calentao","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Colombian cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Calentao', sometimes spelled calenta'o  (Colombian Spanish creole folk slang for \"heated,\" derived from the Standard Spanish word calentado) is a Colombian cuisine dish made from reheated leftovers including rice, egg, pasta, beans, potatoes and other foods such as arepa, chorizo, and ground beef. It is generally eaten for breakfast and is often accompanied by aguapanela, arepa, coffee, juice or hot chocolate.  Depending on the region it can also be served with hogao. It is sometimes referred to as Fríjoles Trasnochaos. A fried egg is usually served on top of the dish and it is called Calentao' \"A Caballo\". It is usually eaten for breakfast using some of the night before's leftovers."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-calentao"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calentao"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Colombian cuisine","cuisine/colombian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"My Colombian Recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"mycolombianrecipes.com","note":"Medellín-born Erica Dinho's blog of traditional Colombian recipes.","url":"https://www.mycolombianrecipes.com/about/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Colombian cuisine","links":[["Arepa","dish/arepa"],["Cholado","dish/cholado"],["Pandebono","dish/pandebono"],["Puchero","dish/puchero"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/5f/Calentao.jpg/500px-Calentao.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Juancplanb","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Calentao.jpg"}},"dish/caruso-sauce":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/caruso-sauce","n":"Caruso sauce","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Uruguayan cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Caruso sauce or salsa Caruso is a warm sauce in Uruguayan cuisine made of cream, ham, cheese, beef extract, and mushrooms, and sometimes nuts or onions. A simpler version is a Béchamel sauce with spices, walnuts, and ham. It is served with pasta, typically cappelletti."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-caruso-sauce"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caruso_sauce"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Uruguayan cuisine","cuisine/uruguayan-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":5,"links":[["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Cream","ingredient/cream"],["Ham","ingredient/ham"]]},{"h":"More from Uruguayan cuisine","links":[["Dulce de batata","dish/dulce-de-batata"],["Puchero","dish/puchero"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/5c/Capelettis_a_la_Caruso.jpg/500px-Capelettis_a_la_Caruso.jpg","w":500,"h":278,"by":"Porfirijuan","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Capelettis_a_la_Caruso.jpg"}},"ingredient/turkey-as-food":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/turkey-as-food","n":"Turkey as food","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 3 dishes across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Turkey meat, commonly referred to simply as turkey, is the meat from turkeys, typically domesticated ones, but also wild turkeys. It is a popular poultry dish, especially in North America and the United Kingdom, where it is traditionally consumed as part of culturally significant events such as Thanksgiving and Christmas as well as in standard cuisine."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-turkey-as-food"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turkey_as_food"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Club sandwich","dish/club-sandwich"]]],["Cuisine of New England","cuisine/cuisine-of-new-england",[["Thanksgiving sandwich","dish/thanksgiving-sandwich"]]],["Mexican cuisine","cuisine/mexican-cuisine",[["Relleno negro","dish/relleno-negro"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Bacon","ingredient/bacon"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Lettuce","ingredient/lettuce"],["Mayonnaise","ingredient/mayonnaise"],["Toast","ingredient/toast"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"],["Turkey meat","ingredient/turkey-meat"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/a/a3/Thanksgiving_Turkey.jpg/500px-Thanksgiving_Turkey.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Patrick Fitzgerald from Atlanta, GA, USA","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl"